1. Flake:
```nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs/nixos-20.09";
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }: {
    packages = {
      my-lib = nixpkgs.callPackage ./my-lib {};
      my-tool = nixpkgs.callPackage ./my-tool {};
    };

    overlay = final: prev: {
      # Override the default package with our custom version
      pkgconfig = prev.pkgconfig // {
        pkg-config = prev.pkgconfig.pkg-config.override { my-lib = self.packages.my-lib; };
      };
    };

    devShell = nixpkgs.devShell.override {
      packages = [ self.packages.my-tool ];
      RUST_SRC_PATH = "${nixpkgs.rust}/lib/rustlib/src";
    };
  };
}
```
This flake defines two custom packages, `my-lib` and `my-tool`, which are built using the `callPackage` function from the `nixpkgs` overlay. The `overlay` section of the flake is used to override the default package with our custom version. The `devShell` section of the flake provides a development shell that exposes both `clang` and `cargo`, with `RUST_SRC_PATH` set properly.

2. Reasoning about fixed-output derivations:
Nix's lazy evaluation interacts with fixed-output derivations in a way that can affect reproducibility with `fetchGit`. When using `fetchGit`, Nix needs to know the exact revision of the Git repository to fetch, as well as any dependencies that are required to build the project. However, if the output of a derivation is not fixed, Nix cannot guarantee that it will produce the same result when rebuilding the project. This is because the output of a derivation can depend on other inputs, such as the environment variables and user input, which may change each time the project is built.

If a derivation is not marked as fixed-output, Nix will attempt to build the derivation every time it is needed, even if the dependencies have not changed. This means that the output of the derivation may be different each time it is built, which can lead to inconsistent results when rebuilding the project.

In contrast, if a derivation is marked as fixed-output, Nix will only attempt to build the derivation when its inputs have changed, and will use the cached result if the inputs have not changed. This means that the output of the derivation will be consistent even if the dependencies have changed, which can help ensure reproducibility with `fetchGit`.

3. Scoping error:
The scoping error in the Nix expression is due to the fact that the `lib` variable is not properly scoped. The `lib` variable is defined within the let expression and can only be accessed within its scope. In order to access the `lib` variable from outside the let expression, it must be explicitly exported using the `export` keyword.

Here is the corrected Nix expression:
```nix
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:
let
  lib = pkgs.lib;
in
  pkgs.stdenv.mkDerivation {
    name = "demo";
    src = ./src;
    buildPhase = ''
      gcc $lib.strings.concatStrings ["hello" "world"]
    '';
  }
```
4. Exposing a formatter output:
To expose a formatter output in the flake, you can add the following to the `outputs` section of the flake:
```nix
formatter = pkgs.alejandra.override { inputs = [ self ]; };
```
This will create a derivation called `formatter` that runs alejandra on all `.nix` files in the project. You can then use this derivation to format your code by running `nix run .#formatter`.

5. Reasoning about binary caches and source:
If nixpkgs only provided binary caches, but no source, it would be impossible for Nix to build any packages from source, as it would not have the source code to build with. In this case, it would be necessary to use a custom overlay that provides the source code for each package. This could be done by adding the `source` attribute to the `callPackage` function in the overlay, which would allow Nix to fetch the source code from a source repository when building the package. For example:
```nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs/nixos-20.09";
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }: {
    # Override the default package with our custom version
    pkgconfig = nixpkgs.pkgconfig // {
      pkg-config = nixpkgs.pkgconfig.pkg-config.override {
        source = fetchGit "https://github.com/my-username/nixpkgs-overrides";
      };
    };
  };
}
```
This overlay would add the `source` attribute to the `pkg-config` package, which would allow Nix to fetch the source code for that package from the specified Git repository. This would allow Nix to build packages from source, even if nixpkgs only provided binary caches and no source.

